###Pure

// risk-dashboard -- what a chief risk officer actually looks at before nine o'clock, and the
// widest fan-in in this graph. Layer 3: it depends on margin-calc, credit-limits and
// pnl-attribution, and on nothing else.
//
// THREE CLOSURES MEET HERE. margin-calc brings position-keeping and risk-core (and through
// them core-instrument, core-account, core-price); credit-limits brings credit-core and
// limit-core (and through them core-party, core-ratings, core-book); pnl-attribution brings
// position-keeping again and valuation-core. So position-keeping, core-account,
// core-instrument and core-price each arrive down TWO paths and must resolve to one of each.
// Nothing below names any of them: every fact this project needs from them is reached through
// one of its three declared dependencies, which is what makes the declared list the true one.
//
// The two constructs this project carries for the graph:
//
//   AGGREGATION  four store Views, each mapped onto a class whose ~primaryKey IS its
//                ~groupBy. One instance of RdbTileDayTotal is a dashboard and a day, never a
//                tile. Three more aggregations are READ rather than declared -- the tiles
//                navigate into margin-calc's MGN_PORTFOLIO_MARGIN_TOTAL and into
//                pnl-attribution's PNL_ACCOUNT_DAY_TOTAL and PNL_INSTRUMENT_DAY_TOTAL -- so a
//                dashboard that shows a book's whole day sums nothing itself.
//
//   VIEW         the same four, declared with ~groupBy in the store. They are Legend views,
//                not database ones: no DDL, nothing seeds them, and the engine folds the
//                GROUP BY into the SQL. An owner with no exceptions on a date forms NO GROUP
//                and is absent, which on this dashboard is the right answer: an empty queue
//                is not a queue.
//
// The modelling claim, which is the whole design:
//
//   A DASHBOARD TILE IS A QUERY WITH A THRESHOLD AND AN OWNER.
//
// A number on a screen that nobody owns and nothing compares against is decoration. So
// RdbTile carries the measure and the source it reads, RdbThresholdPolicy carries the amber
// and red levels and which direction is bad, RdbOwner carries the person who has to do
// something, and RdbTileSnapshot is what that tile said on one morning. When a snapshot goes
// red it raises an RdbException, which is a row with a due time, an escalation chain and a
// decision -- because the exceptions that need a decision today are the only part of a risk
// dashboard that is not a report.
//
// The six panels, which are the six things a CRO reads in order:
//
//   1  limit utilisation and its breaches      RdbLimitPanelRow, RdbLimitBreachRow
//   2  margin calls outstanding                RdbMarginCallRow, RdbMarginShortfallRow
//   3  P&L against its explain                 RdbExplainRow, RdbResidualRow
//   4  the largest movers                      RdbMoverRow
//   5  the concentrations                      RdbConcentrationRow
//   6  the exceptions needing a decision       RdbException and its three subtypes
//
// and two roll-ups that only exist because all three sources are present at once:
// RdbCounterpartyRollUp (one name's limit, margin and P&L in one row) and RdbDeskRollUp.
//
// Prefixes per the project contract: classes Rdb*, tables RDB_*, joins Rdb_*, filters Rdb*,
// set ids rdb*.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the board

// FOR: the board itself -- which tiles are on it, who it is for, and what currency its
// numbers are stated in. A firm has several: the CRO's, the treasurer's, the desk head's, and
// they differ in which tiles they carry rather than in where the numbers come from.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbDashboard
[
   tileCountNotNegative: ($this.tileCount >= 0)
]
{
   // The key.
   dashboardId: String[1];
   dashboardName: String[1];
   // CRO, TREASURER, DESK_HEAD, PRODUCT_CONTROL. Who this board is laid out for.
   audienceRole: String[1];
   // The person accountable for the board being right, as distinct from the tile owners.
   ownerId: String[1];
   // When it refreshes, as the scheduler states it.
   refreshCron: String[1];
   // ISO 4217 every money tile on it is converted to.
   reportingCurrency: String[1];
   // SMALLINT: how many tiles it carries, denormalised for the header.
   tileCount: Integer[1];
   // True for the board opened when nobody chose one.
   isDefault: Boolean[1];
   // Absent while the board is in use. A null test, which is what the store filter screens on.
   retiredOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   // Whether anyone still opens it.
   isLive() { $this.retiredOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];

   owner: risk_dashboard::RdbOwner[0..1];
   tiles: risk_dashboard::RdbTile[*];
   // INTO THE AGGREGATION: one row per day, never a tile.
   dayTotals: risk_dashboard::RdbTileDayTotal[*];
}

// FOR: **the central claim of this project** -- a tile is a QUERY with a THRESHOLD and an
// OWNER. The query is the measure and the source binding; the threshold is the policy it is
// compared against; the owner is who has to act when it breaches. Drop any of the three and
// the tile is decoration: a number with no comparison cannot be wrong, and one with no owner
// cannot be fixed.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbTile
[
   sortOrderNotNegative: ($this.sortOrder >= 0),
   // A tile whose data may be infinitely old is not being monitored.
   maxAgeIsPositive: ($this.maxAgeMinutes > 0)
]
{
   // The key.
   tileId: String[1];
   // The board it sits on.
   dashboardId: String[1];
   // A stable short code, so a tile can be referred to in a policy document.
   tileCode: String[1];
   // What the tile is called on the screen.
   title: String[1];
   // Which of the six panels it belongs to: LIMIT, MARGIN, EXPLAIN, MOVER, CONCENTRATION,
   // EXCEPTION.
   panelCode: String[1];
   // The measure the query returns, e.g. UTILISATION_PCT or OUTSTANDING_CALL_AMOUNT.
   measureCode: String[1];
   // AMOUNT, PCT, COUNT or RATIO -- what the headline number IS, without which a threshold
   // cannot be compared to it.
   unitCode: String[1];
   // THE THRESHOLD, by reference. Policies are shared across tiles on purpose.
   policyCode: String[1];
   // THE OWNER, by reference.
   ownerId: String[1];
   // SMALLINT: where it appears on the page. Order is a risk statement, not a layout choice.
   sortOrder: Integer[1];
   // SMALLINT: how stale the underlying data may be before the tile itself is a problem.
   maxAgeMinutes: Integer[1];
   // True when a red on this tile stops the morning sign-off rather than merely appearing.
   isBlocking: Boolean[1];
   // Absent while the tile is on the board.
   retiredOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   isLive() { $this.retiredOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // The tile as one string, for a report header that has no room for three columns.
   tileLabel() { $this.panelCode + '/' + $this.tileCode } : String[1];

   dashboard: risk_dashboard::RdbDashboard[0..1];
   owner: risk_dashboard::RdbOwner[0..1];
   policy: risk_dashboard::RdbThresholdPolicy[0..1];
   // THE QUERY HALF: which upstream elements this tile actually reads.
   bindings: risk_dashboard::RdbSourceBinding[*];
   snapshots: risk_dashboard::RdbTileSnapshot[*];
   commentary: risk_dashboard::RdbCommentary[*];
   exceptions: risk_dashboard::RdbException[*];
}

// FOR: the threshold half of a tile, held once and shared -- the amber level, the red level,
// and WHICH DIRECTION IS BAD, because a utilisation of 95% is red and a collateral coverage
// of 95% is also red and they are not the same comparison. The absolute floor stops a
// percentage rule raising an exception on a trivially small number.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbThresholdPolicy
[
   // Amber is the earlier warning, so on an ABOVE policy it sits below red. Stated as a
   // separation rather than an ordering, because a BELOW policy inverts it.
   levelsAreDistinct: (($this.redLevel - $this.amberLevel)->abs() > 0.0),
   escalationLevelIsPositive: ($this.escalationLevel >= 1),
   responseIsPositive: ($this.responseMinutes > 0)
]
{
   // The key.
   policyCode: String[1];
   policyName: String[1];
   // ABOVE (bigger is worse) or BELOW (smaller is worse). The direction of the comparison.
   direction: String[1];
   // The warning level.
   amberLevel: Float[1];
   // The level that raises an exception.
   redLevel: Float[1];
   // Beneath this amount the percentage rule is not applied at all: a 200% breach of a
   // hundred-unit limit is not the CRO's morning.
   absoluteFloor: Float[1];
   // SMALLINT: 1 desk, 2 business, 3 CRO. How far up a red goes on its own.
   escalationLevel: Integer[1];
   // SMALLINT: how long the owner has before it escalates further.
   responseMinutes: Integer[1];
   // True for the fallback used by a tile with no more specific policy.
   isDefault: Boolean[1];

   // The width of the amber band -- how much warning the policy actually gives.
   bandWidth() { ($this.redLevel - $this.amberLevel)->abs() } : Float[1];
   // Whether a value the caller supplies is red under THIS policy, direction included. The
   // one place the direction is interpreted, so no tile has to decide it again.
   isRedAt(value: Float[1]) { if($this.direction == 'ABOVE', |$value >= $this.redLevel, |$value <= $this.redLevel) } : Boolean[1];
   // And whether it is at least amber.
   isAmberAt(value: Float[1]) { if($this.direction == 'ABOVE', |$value >= $this.amberLevel, |$value <= $this.amberLevel) } : Boolean[1];

   tiles: risk_dashboard::RdbTile[*];
}

// FOR: the owner half of a tile and of every exception it raises -- the named human or role
// that has to do something today. An exception assigned to a team is assigned to nobody.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbOwner
{
   // The key.
   ownerId: String[1];
   ownerName: String[1];
   // CRO, CREDIT_OFFICER, MARGIN_OPS, PRODUCT_CONTROL, DESK_HEAD.
   roleCode: String[1];
   // The desk they sit on, where they sit on one.
   deskCode: String[0..1];
   // Who this owner escalates to. Held as an id rather than a navigation: the chain is data
   // that changes on a rota and is read upwards, one level at a time.
   escalatesToId: String[0..1];
   contactEmail: String[1];
   // True for the person actually reachable this morning, which is not always the owner.
   isOnCall: Boolean[1];

   // Whether there is anywhere above this owner to go.
   hasEscalation() { $this.escalatesToId->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // The owner as one string for a queue header.
   ownerLabel() { $this.roleCode + ':' + $this.ownerName } : String[1];

   tiles: risk_dashboard::RdbTile[*];
   dashboards: risk_dashboard::RdbDashboard[*];
   exceptions: risk_dashboard::RdbException[*];
   // INTO THE AGGREGATION: this owner's whole queue on a day, as one row.
   queueTotals: risk_dashboard::RdbOwnerQueueTotal[*];
}

// FOR: the query half of a tile, stated as data -- which project, which element, and which
// expression the tile's number came out of. This is the provenance row: without it "limit
// utilisation" on a screen is a number whose definition lives in somebody's head, and the
// first argument about it cannot be settled.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbSourceBinding
[
   sourceSeqIsPositive: ($this.sourceSeq >= 1)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   tileId: String[1];
   // Part 2: a tile may read more than one source, and a tile that reads three is exactly
   // what this project is for.
   sourceSeq: Integer[1];
   // margin-calc, credit-limits or pnl-attribution. The only three legal values, because
   // they are the only three declared dependencies.
   sourceProject: String[1];
   // The element read, e.g. margin_calc::MgnMarginCall or pnl_attribution::PnlAccountDayTotal.
   sourceElement: String[1];
   // CLASS, VIEW or FUNCTION -- whether the tile reads rows, reads a GROUP BY somebody else
   // declared, or calls a rule somebody else owns.
   sourceKind: String[1];
   // The expression, as published in the data dictionary.
   measureExpression: String[1];
   // True for the binding the headline number comes from; the others are context.
   isPrimary: Boolean[1];

   // The element as one fully-qualified string.
   provenance() { $this.sourceProject + ' -> ' + $this.sourceElement } : String[1];
   // Whether this binding reads an aggregation rather than rows. A tile bound to a view
   // costs one grouped read and cannot drift from the group it is showing.
   readsAnAggregate() { $this.sourceKind == 'VIEW' } : Boolean[1];

   tile: risk_dashboard::RdbTile[0..1];
}

// FOR: what a tile SAID on one morning -- the number, the two thresholds it was judged
// against, and the verdict. Its own row per day rather than a mutable current value, because
// a dashboard that cannot be replayed cannot be argued with, and every morning meeting is
// eventually an argument about what the screen showed.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbTileSnapshot
[
   exceptionCountNotNegative: ($this.exceptionCount >= 0),
   lagNotNegative: ($this.refreshLagMinutes >= 0)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   tileId: String[1];
   // Part 2. One snapshot per tile per business date.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Denormalised from the tile so the day view can group on it without a join.
   dashboardId: String[1];
   // Which morning run produced it.
   runSeq: Integer[1];
   // THE NUMBER. Whatever the tile's unitCode says it is.
   headlineValue: Float[1];
   // Yesterday's, carried forward, so a move is visible without reading another row.
   priorValue: Float[1];
   // The two levels, copied off the policy so a later re-calibration does not silently
   // re-judge a closed morning.
   amberThreshold: Float[1];
   redThreshold: Float[1];
   // headlineValue / redThreshold as computed. Above 1.0 on an ABOVE policy is a red.
   breachRatio: Float[1];
   // The money this tile puts at risk today -- an uncollateralised shortfall, an unapproved
   // excess, an unexplained residual. Zero for a tile that is a count.
   amountAtRisk: Float[1];
   // SMALLINT: how many exceptions this tile raised this morning.
   exceptionCount: Integer[1];
   // GREEN, AMBER or RED as the run recorded it.
   statusCode: String[1];
   // SMALLINT: how old the underlying data was when the tile was computed.
   refreshLagMinutes: Integer[1];
   // How many source rows it stood on. A tile computed from nothing is green and wrong.
   sourceRowCount: Integer[1];
   computedAt: DateTime[1];

   // The move since yesterday, in the tile's own unit.
   dayMove() { $this.headlineValue - $this.priorValue } : Float[1];
   // The same as a fraction of yesterday. `/` widens to Float regardless of its operands.
   dayMovePct() { ($this.headlineValue - $this.priorValue) / $this.priorValue } : Float[1];
   // The verdict re-derived from the two thresholds rather than read off statusCode -- the
   // data-quality screen on the run's own colouring.
   derivedStatus() { if($this.headlineValue >= $this.redThreshold, |'RED', |if($this.headlineValue >= $this.amberThreshold, |'AMBER', |'GREEN')) } : String[1];
   // Whether the tile agrees with itself.
   statusIsConsistent() { $this.statusCode == if($this.headlineValue >= $this.redThreshold, |'RED', |if($this.headlineValue >= $this.amberThreshold, |'AMBER', |'GREEN')) } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether the number is old enough that the tile is reporting on the past. Read from the
   // TILE's tolerance, not from a constant here.
   isStale() { $this.refreshLagMinutes > $this.tile->toOne().maxAgeMinutes } : Boolean[1];
   // A tile computed from no rows at all. Green because there was nothing to be red about,
   // which is the single most dangerous state on a risk dashboard.
   isEmptyGreen() { ($this.sourceRowCount == 0) && ($this.statusCode == 'GREEN') } : Boolean[1];
   // What share of this tile's exceptions this morning are the whole day's amount at risk.
   averageExceptionSize() { $this.amountAtRisk / $this.exceptionCount } : Float[1];

   tile: risk_dashboard::RdbTile[0..1];
   run: risk_dashboard::RdbMorningRun[0..1];
   exceptions: risk_dashboard::RdbException[*];
   // INTO THE AGGREGATION.
   dayTotal: risk_dashboard::RdbTileDayTotal[0..1];
}

// FOR: **the fan-in, declared once at batch level.** One refresh of the board names the
// margin run, the credit report and the P&L attribution run it read -- all three, by their
// own keys, on one row. Three upstream batches that know nothing about each other are
// reconciled here or nowhere, and a dashboard built from a complete margin run, yesterday's
// credit report and a failed attribution is a dashboard that looks fine.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbMorningRun
[
   tileCountIsPositive: ($this.tileCount > 0),
   // The three colours partition the tiles, so they cannot exceed them.
   coloursWithinTiles: (($this.greenCount + $this.amberCount + $this.redCount) <= $this.tileCount)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key: the business date the board is showing.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Part 2: which refresh on that date. The 07:00 build and the 11:00 rebuild are both kept.
   runSeq: Integer[1];
   // SOURCE 1 -- margin-calc. Its run is keyed (COB_DATE, RUN_SEQ), so only the sequence is
   // needed beside our own date.
   marginRunSeq: Integer[1];
   // SOURCE 2 -- credit-limits. Its daily report is keyed by a report id alone.
   creditReportId: String[1];
   // SOURCE 3 -- pnl-attribution. Its run is keyed by an attribution id alone.
   attributionId: String[1];
   // EOD, INTRADAY or REBUILD.
   runType: String[1];
   // PENDING, RUNNING, COMPLETE, FAILED, PARTIAL. PARTIAL is the one that matters: it means
   // fewer than three sources arrived.
   status: String[1];
   // SMALLINT: the shape of the board this morning.
   tileCount: Integer[1];
   greenCount: Integer[1];
   amberCount: Integer[1];
   redCount: Integer[1];
   // How many exceptions the whole refresh raised.
   exceptionCount: Integer[1];
   // The money the board puts at risk, totalled over its tiles.
   totalAmountAtRisk: Float[1];
   startedAt: DateTime[1];
   completedAt: DateTime[0..1];

   isComplete() { $this.status == 'COMPLETE' } : Boolean[1];
   // Share of the board that is red. Integer / Integer widens to Float on its own.
   redPct() { $this.redCount / $this.tileCount } : Float[1];
   // Share that is not green, which is what the CRO actually counts.
   notGreenPct() { ($this.amberCount + $this.redCount) / $this.tileCount } : Float[1];
   // THE FAN-IN TEST. All three upstream batches resolved, or the board is showing a mixture
   // of this morning and some other one. Each navigation is [0..1] into a different project.
   allThreeSourcesResolved() { ($this.marginRun->isNotEmpty() && $this.creditReport->isNotEmpty()) && $this.attributionRun->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // The three source keys as one string, for the header of a board that has to be explained
   // to somebody a week later.
   sourceLabel() { $this.creditReportId + ' | ' + $this.attributionId + ' | ' + $this.marginRunSeq->toString() } : String[1];
   // Whether the P&L half was signed off before the board published it. A red board built on
   // an unsigned run is a red board about the run and not about the risk.
   pnlSignedOff() { $this.attributionRun->toOne().isSignedOff } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether the margin half finished. margin-calc states completeness on its status column.
   marginRunComplete() { $this.marginRun->toOne().isComplete() } : Boolean[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT, ALL THREE, ON ONE CLASS. This is the widest fan-in in the graph, and it
   // is three property mappings.
   marginRun: margin_calc::MgnMarginRun[0..1];
   creditReport: credit_limits::ClmDailyLimitReport[0..1];
   attributionRun: pnl_attribution::PnlAttributionRun[0..1];

   snapshots: risk_dashboard::RdbTileSnapshot[*];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- panel 1: the limits

// FOR: one credit limit's utilisation as the board shows it this morning -- the first thing
// on the page, because a limit is a promise the firm made and utilisation is how close it is
// to breaking it. The numbers are FROZEN onto this row rather than navigated for, so the
// board can be reprinted; `utilisationLine` is the live source and the drift between them is
// the screen on whether the board is stale.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbLimitPanelRow
[
   appliedThresholdIsPositive: ($this.appliedThreshold > 0.0),
   utilisedNotNegative: ($this.utilisedAmount >= 0.0),
   // A percentage may pass 100 -- that is what a breach is -- but not go negative.
   pctNotNegative: ($this.utilisationPct >= 0.0)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Part 2: credit-limits' own anchor id.
   creditLimitId: String[1];
   // The counterparty, carried so the counterparty roll-up can group on it without a join.
   counterpartyId: String[1];
   // The obligor and the limit-core triple, carried for the same reason: a board row that
   // cannot say WHOSE limit it is has to be joined before it can be read.
   obligorId: String[1];
   bookId: String[1];
   measureCode: String[1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8), as published this morning.
   utilisedAmount: Float[1];
   appliedThreshold: Float[1];
   // Negative here is an excess, so it deliberately carries no non-negative constraint.
   headroomAmount: Float[1];
   utilisationPct: Float[1];
   // The agency symbol in force, frozen. A downgrade is the commonest cause of a breach
   // nobody traded into, and the frozen symbol is how that is told from trading.
   ratingSymbol: String[0..1];
   currencyCode: String[1];
   // SMALLINT: how many exposures rolled into the number.
   contributorCount: Integer[1];
   isBreached: Boolean[1];
   // SMALLINT: where this row sits when the panel is sorted worst-first.
   rankPosition: Integer[1];
   // The tile this row appears under.
   tileId: String[1];

   // The ratio, from the two numbers on this row. Float[1] because `/` widens.
   utilisationRatio() { $this.utilisedAmount / $this.appliedThreshold } : Float[1];
   // What is left as a percentage. Below zero when the limit is over.
   headroomPct() { 100.0 - $this.utilisationPct } : Float[1];
   isOverLimit() { $this.utilisedAmount > $this.appliedThreshold } : Boolean[1];
   // THE STALENESS SCREEN: what the board printed, less what credit-limits says now. Any
   // non-zero here means the board and the source have diverged since the run.
   utilisationDrift() { $this.utilisedAmount - $this.utilisationLine.utilisedAmount->toOne() } : Float[1];
   // This limit's share of everything the same counterparty is using, read out of our own
   // aggregation rather than by summing the other rows.
   shareOfCounterparty() { $this.utilisedAmount / $this.counterpartyTotal.totalUtilised->toOne() } : Float[1];

   // ROUTE 1 THROUGH credit-limits' MILESTONING -- its own <<temporal.businesstemporal>>
   // ClmLimitStanding, wrapped twice: once by credit-limits as approvedThresholdOn, once
   // here. The dated form is compulsory; `$l.standing` with no date does not compile.
   // Returns Float[*] because a business date can select a span that is not there.
   approvedThresholdOn(businessDate: Date[1])
   {
      $this.creditLimit->toOne().approvedThresholdOn($businessDate)
   } : Float[*];

   // ROUTE 2, WHICH IS SOMEBODY ELSE'S MILESTONING -- through credit-limits, through
   // credit-core, to core-ratings' milestoned rating version. String[*] and not a rating
   // object: core-ratings is transitive here and is deliberately never named.
   ratingSymbolOn(businessDate: Date[1])
   {
      $this.creditLimit->toOne().ratingSymbolOn($businessDate)
   } : String[*];

   // CROSS-PROJECT: the live utilisation line, on (limit, date), and the limit itself.
   utilisationLine: credit_limits::ClmUtilisationLine[0..1];
   creditLimit: credit_limits::ClmCreditLimit[0..1];
   tile: risk_dashboard::RdbTile[0..1];
   // INTO OUR OWN AGGREGATION.
   counterpartyTotal: risk_dashboard::RdbCounterpartyLimitTotal[0..1];
}

// FOR: a limit breach as an item on the board rather than as a credit record -- with the age,
// the owner and whether it needs a decision TODAY, none of which credit-limits holds because
// none of them is a fact about the excess. The excess is what happened; this is the queue.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbLimitBreachRow
[
   excessNotNegative: ($this.excessAmount >= 0.0),
   severityInRange: (($this.severity >= 1) && ($this.severity <= 5)),
   ageNotNegative: ($this.ageDays >= 0)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Part 2: credit-limits' excess id.
   excessId: String[1];
   creditLimitId: String[1];
   counterpartyId: String[1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8): the threshold crossed, the usage seen, the gap, and how much of the gap
   // has actually been signed for.
   breachedThreshold: Float[1];
   observedAmount: Float[1];
   excessAmount: Float[1];
   approvedAmount: Float[1];
   // SMALLINT: 1 to 5, copied from the excess.
   severity: Integer[1];
   // LOW, MED, HIGH, CRIT -- the escalation ladder's own word for the same thing.
   severityCode: String[1];
   // The escalation ticket credit-limits raised, where it raised one.
   ticketId: String[0..1];
   // SMALLINT: how many days this breach has been open. The number that turns a breach into
   // an escalation without anything else changing.
   ageDays: Integer[1];
   currencyCode: String[1];
   ownerId: String[1];
   detectedAt: DateTime[1];
   // Absent while the breach is open, which is how the open-breach filter is written.
   clearedOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   isOpen() { $this.clearedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   isCritical() { $this.severity >= 4 } : Boolean[1];
   // What is still unapproved -- the number that decides whether this escalates tonight.
   unapprovedAmount() { $this.excessAmount - $this.approvedAmount } : Float[1];
   // The size of the breach relative to the limit it broke.
   excessRatio() { $this.excessAmount / $this.breachedThreshold } : Float[1];
   // THE MORNING QUESTION. Open, material, and either critical or older than a day: this is
   // the row that needs a decision before lunch.
   needsDecisionToday() { ($this.clearedOn->isEmpty() && ($this.excessAmount > $this.approvedAmount)) && (($this.severity >= 4) || ($this.ageDays >= 1)) } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether the board's severity still agrees with the source's.
   severityAgrees() { $this.severity == $this.excess.severity->toOne() } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether anybody has answered the escalation. Read from credit-limits' ticket, which is
   // where the answer actually lands.
   escalationOutstanding() { $this.ticket->toOne().isOutstanding() } : Boolean[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT.
   excess: credit_limits::ClmExcess[0..1];
   ticket: credit_limits::ClmEscalationTicket[0..1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- panel 2: the margin

// FOR: the margin calls outstanding this morning -- one row per agreement, with what was
// demanded, what the MTA suppressed, and whether the collateral has turned up. The
// suppressed amount is on the board on purpose: it is uncollateralised exposure that appears
// on no call report, and margin-calc goes to some trouble to keep it separate.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbMarginCallRow
[
   requiredNotNegative: ($this.requiredMargin >= 0.0),
   mtaNotNegative: ($this.minimumTransferAmount >= 0.0)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Part 2: margin-calc's agreement id.
   agreementId: String[1];
   counterpartyId: String[1];
   // DELIVER or RETURN.
   callDirection: String[1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8), copied off the call so the board can be reprinted.
   requiredMargin: Float[1];
   collateralValue: Float[1];
   threshold: Float[1];
   minimumTransferAmount: Float[1];
   // The shortfall that exists, and the amount that may be demanded. Two columns, because
   // margin-calc keeps them apart and collapsing them here would undo that.
   grossCallAmount: Float[1];
   callAmount: Float[1];
   callCurrency: String[1];
   isCallMade: Boolean[1];
   isSettled: Boolean[1];
   // Absent unless the counterparty is arguing.
   disputeCode: String[0..1];
   // SMALLINT: hours since the notice went out. A call unsettled past its deadline is the
   // margin panel's only real alarm.
   ageHours: Integer[1];
   // SMALLINT: the contractual settlement deadline, in hours from issue.
   deadlineHours: Integer[1];
   ownerId: String[1];
   rankPosition: Integer[1];

   // The shortfall from first principles, using MARGIN-CALC'S OWN FUNCTION rather than
   // subtracting the threshold here -- so the board cannot deduct it twice.
   recomputedShortfall() { margin_calc::mgnCallAmount($this.requiredMargin, $this.collateralValue, $this.threshold) } : Float[1];
   // THE MTA TEST, also margin-calc's.
   meetsMinimumTransfer() { margin_calc::mgnMeetsMta($this.grossCallAmount, $this.minimumTransferAmount) } : Boolean[1];
   // Exposure left standing BECAUSE of the MTA: the whole gross when suppressed, nothing when
   // called. The number the margin panel exists to show, and the one the call report cannot.
   uncollateralisedByMta() { if(margin_calc::mgnMeetsMta($this.grossCallAmount, $this.minimumTransferAmount), |0.0, |$this.grossCallAmount) } : Float[1];
   // Held against required. Below 1.0 is a shortfall.
   coverageRatio() { $this.collateralValue / $this.requiredMargin } : Float[1];
   // Called and not yet settled -- the definition of outstanding.
   isOutstanding() { $this.isCallMade && $this.isSettled->not() } : Boolean[1];
   // Outstanding past its deadline.
   isOverdue() { ($this.isCallMade && $this.isSettled->not()) && ($this.ageHours > $this.deadlineHours) } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether the board's figure still matches margin-calc's. Non-zero means the board is
   // showing a number the margin system has since revised.
   callDrift() { $this.callAmount - $this.call.callAmount->toOne() } : Float[1];
   // The agreement's own trigger level, read rather than re-derived: threshold plus MTA.
   callTriggerLevel() { $this.agreement->toOne().callTriggerLevel() } : Float[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT.
   call: margin_calc::MgnMarginCall[0..1];
   agreement: margin_calc::MgnMarginAgreement[0..1];
}

// FOR: the initial margin required of a netting set against the collateral actually sitting
// there -- the panel's second row, and the one that says whether a call is COMING rather than
// whether one was made. It reads margin-calc's portfolio aggregation for the set's whole day
// instead of summing positions.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbMarginShortfallRow
[
   horizonIsPositive: ($this.closeOutPeriodDays > 0),
   marginNotNegative: ($this.totalInitialMargin >= 0.0)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Part 2: margin-calc's netting set.
   marginPortfolioId: String[1];
   // Part 3: the model. The regulatory number and the internal one are both on the board,
   // because the difference between them is itself a risk statement.
   methodCode: String[1];
   agreementId: String[1];
   counterpartyId: String[1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8): the four components of the charge, copied off margin-calc.
   oneDayMargin: Float[1];
   scaledMargin: Float[1];
   concentrationAddOn: Float[1];
   liquidityAddOn: Float[1];
   totalInitialMargin: Float[1];
   // SMALLINT: the margin period of risk this row was scaled to.
   closeOutPeriodDays: Integer[1];
   // What is actually pledged, after haircut.
   collateralHeld: Float[1];
   // Required less held. Negative is an excess of collateral, which is also worth seeing.
   shortfallAmount: Float[1];
   imCurrency: String[1];
   rankPosition: Integer[1];

   // Both add-ons together.
   addOnTotal() { $this.concentrationAddOn + $this.liquidityAddOn } : Float[1];
   // What share of the charge is add-on rather than model. A large share means the model is
   // being corrected rather than used.
   addOnPct() { ($this.concentrationAddOn + $this.liquidityAddOn) / $this.scaledMargin } : Float[1];
   // THE WHOLE CALCULATION, from margin-calc's function: scale the one-day number to the
   // close-out period, then stack the add-ons -- which are NOT scaled a second time.
   marginFromModel() { margin_calc::mgnTotalInitialMargin($this.oneDayMargin, $this.closeOutPeriodDays, $this.concentrationAddOn, $this.liquidityAddOn) } : Float[1];
   // The gap between what the board shows and what the rule produces. Should be zero.
   modelDrift() { $this.totalInitialMargin - margin_calc::mgnTotalInitialMargin($this.oneDayMargin, $this.closeOutPeriodDays, $this.concentrationAddOn, $this.liquidityAddOn) } : Float[1];
   // The square-root-of-time multiplier this row's horizon implies, also margin-calc's.
   horizonScalar() { margin_calc::mgnCloseOutScalar($this.closeOutPeriodDays) } : Float[1];
   coverageRatio() { $this.collateralHeld / $this.totalInitialMargin } : Float[1];
   isUndercollateralised() { $this.collateralHeld < $this.totalInitialMargin } : Boolean[1];
   // READING SOMEBODY ELSE'S AGGREGATION: this method's charge against the set's whole
   // margin for the day, out of margin-calc's own GROUP BY. Nothing here sums a position.
   shareOfPortfolioMargin() { $this.totalInitialMargin / $this.portfolioTotal.totalInitialMargin->toOne() } : Float[1];
   // And whether that whole set is really one position wearing a portfolio's clothes.
   isSinglePositionDriven() { $this.portfolioTotal->toOne().isSinglePositionDriven() } : Boolean[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT: the row, and the GROUP.
   initialMargin: margin_calc::MgnInitialMargin[0..1];
   portfolioTotal: margin_calc::MgnPortfolioMarginTotal[0..1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- panel 3: the explain

// FOR: one book's P&L set against the explain of it -- what the desk made, what six causes
// account for, and the gap. The gap is the number risk management reads, and it is on the
// board next to the P&L rather than on a separate page, because a large P&L that is fully
// explained and a small one that is not are opposite kinds of morning.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbExplainRow
[
   lineCountIsPositive: ($this.lineCount > 0),
   brokenWithinLines: ($this.brokenLineCount <= $this.lineCount)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key: pnl-attribution's run.
   attributionId: String[1];
   // Parts 2 and 3: the account, which is TWO columns everywhere the account appears.
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   // Part 4.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // What the desk is called on the board.
   deskCode: String[1];
   deskName: String[1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8): the day, as published.
   totalPnl: Float[1];
   // What the six causes claim, summed.
   explainedPnl: Float[1];
   // What they failed to claim. Stored AND derivable, and the two must agree.
   unexplainedAmount: Float[1];
   unexplainedPct: Float[1];
   // SMALLINT: the shape of the run behind the row.
   lineCount: Integer[1];
   brokenLineCount: Integer[1];
   reportingCurrency: String[1];
   isSignedOff: Boolean[1];
   ownerId: String[1];
   rankPosition: Integer[1];

   // The residual from the two numbers on this row.
   residualFromRow() { $this.totalPnl - $this.explainedPnl } : Float[1];
   // How much of the day the explain covered. The screen a reviewer sorts on.
   explainQuality() { $this.explainedPnl / $this.totalPnl } : Float[1];
   // Share of lines that breached their tolerance. Integer / Integer widens on its own.
   brokenPct() { $this.brokenLineCount / $this.lineCount } : Float[1];
   // READING SOMEBODY ELSE'S AGGREGATION: pnl-attribution's own account-day GROUP, and its
   // own definition of unexplained. The board does not re-derive a residual; it asks.
   unexplainedFromSource() { $this.accountTotal->toOne().totalUnexplained() } : Float[1];
   // The gap between the two answers. Non-zero means the board is stale or the run was
   // re-attributed after publication, and either is worth a decision.
   explainDrift() { $this.unexplainedAmount - $this.accountTotal->toOne().totalUnexplained() } : Float[1];
   // The book's return on its opening value, also out of the group.
   returnPct() { $this.accountTotal->toOne().returnPct() } : Float[1];
   // Whether the run behind the row is even finished.
   runIsComplete() { $this.attributionRun->toOne().isComplete() } : Boolean[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT: the GROUP, and the run above it.
   accountTotal: pnl_attribution::PnlAccountDayTotal[0..1];
   attributionRun: pnl_attribution::PnlAttributionRun[0..1];
}

// FOR: one unexplained residual that has been given an owner and a deadline -- the P&L half
// of the decision queue. pnl-attribution holds the number and the break; this holds what has
// to happen about it today, which is not a fact about the P&L.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbResidualRow
[
   severityInRange: (($this.severity >= 1) && ($this.severity <= 5)),
   toleranceIsPositive: ($this.tolerancePct > 0.0)
]
{
   // Five key columns -- pnl-attribution's whole line grain, repeated exactly. Anything
   // shorter merges runs, accounts, securities or dates.
   attributionId: String[1];
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // The break pnl-attribution raised, where it raised one. Null when the residual is inside
   // tolerance and the board is showing it for size rather than for action.
   breakCode: String[0..1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8): the residual and the tolerance it was judged against, frozen.
   residualAmount: Float[1];
   residualPct: Float[1];
   tolerancePct: Float[1];
   toleranceAmount: Float[1];
   // SMALLINT: 1 informational, 5 blocks the sign-off.
   severity: Integer[1];
   assignedTo: String[0..1];
   resolvedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   currencyCode: String[1];

   // How far past tolerance it went. Above 1.0 is a breach.
   breachRatio() { $this.residualPct / $this.tolerancePct } : Float[1];
   isOpen() { $this.resolvedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // A break that stops the whole run being signed off. The only tile on this board that can
   // block a publication.
   isBlocking() { $this.severity >= 5 } : Boolean[1];
   // A residual nobody has been given. The state this project exists to make visible.
   isUnowned() { $this.assignedTo->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether the board's residual still matches what pnl-attribution's run wrote.
   residualDrift() { $this.residualAmount - $this.residual.residualAmount->toOne() } : Float[1];
   // And whether the source thinks it has been accounted for, which the board may not know.
   explainedAtSource() { $this.residual->toOne().isExplained() } : Boolean[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT: the number, and the obligation it created.
   residual: pnl_attribution::PnlResidual[0..1];
   attributionBreak: pnl_attribution::PnlAttributionBreak[0..1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- panel 4: the movers

// FOR: the largest movers -- one security across every account, ranked by how much the firm's
// day turned on it. The grain is deliberately the SECURITY and not the position: a name that
// moved the firm through six books is one story, and reading it off the position table makes
// it six. It reads pnl-attribution's instrument-day group rather than scanning lines.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbMoverRow
[
   rankIsPositive: ($this.rankPosition >= 1)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   attributionId: String[1];
   // Part 2: the security.
   instrumentId: String[1];
   // Part 3.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // What it is called on the board.
   instrumentName: String[1];
   assetClass: String[1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8): the two ends of its day and the move between them.
   openingValue: Float[1];
   closingValue: Float[1];
   moveAmount: Float[1];
   movePct: Float[1];
   // SMALLINT: how many accounts held it -- one book or twelve, which changes what to do.
   accountCount: Integer[1];
   // SMALLINT: rank on the panel, worst-first.
   rankPosition: Integer[1];
   // UP or DOWN. Kept as a column because the panel is sorted on size and read on direction.
   direction: String[1];
   currencyCode: String[1];

   // Size without direction: a mover is a mover in either sign.
   absoluteMove() { $this.moveAmount->abs() } : Float[1];
   // The move re-derived from the two ends -- the data-quality screen on moveAmount.
   moveFromEnds() { $this.closingValue - $this.openingValue } : Float[1];
   isTopTen() { $this.rankPosition <= 10 } : Boolean[1];
   // Concentrated in one book, which makes it a desk conversation rather than a firm one.
   isSingleBook() { $this.accountCount == 1 } : Boolean[1];
   // READING SOMEBODY ELSE'S AGGREGATION: the same security's day out of pnl-attribution's
   // instrument-day GROUP, and its own split between market and activity.
   moveFromSource() { $this.instrumentTotal->toOne().valueChange() } : Float[1];
   // How much of the security's day was the market moving rather than the firm trading.
   marketShare() { $this.instrumentTotal->toOne().marketShare() } : Float[1];
   // A mover whose day will not reconcile across the accounts holding it is a data problem
   // and not a trading one, and this is where that is told apart.
   unexplainedOnSecurity() { $this.instrumentTotal->toOne().totalUnexplained() } : Float[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT, INTO A GROUP.
   instrumentTotal: pnl_attribution::PnlInstrumentDayTotal[0..1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- panel 5: concentration

// FOR: the concentrations -- where one netting set holds so much of one risk factor that the
// model's own close-out assumption stops being true. margin-calc computes the charge; the
// board shows it against the FIRM's whole delta in that factor, which margin-calc reads out
// of risk-core's aggregation, so this row reaches a group two projects away without summing
// a single sensitivity leg.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbConcentrationRow
[
   thresholdIsPositive: ($this.concentrationThreshold > 0.0),
   rankIsPositive: ($this.rankPosition >= 1)
]
{
   // Four key columns, margin-calc's grain exactly.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   marginPortfolioId: String[1];
   riskClass: String[1];
   factorId: String[1];
   counterpartyId: String[1];
   // What the factor is called on the board.
   factorName: String[1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8): the netted sensitivity, signed, and the threshold it is measured against.
   netSensitivity: Float[1];
   concentrationThreshold: Float[1];
   concentrationRatio: Float[1];
   // The money the concentration costs.
   addOnAmount: Float[1];
   addOnCurrency: String[1];
   drivingBucketCode: String[1];
   isBreaching: Boolean[1];
   rankPosition: Integer[1];

   // The ratio on SIZE rather than direction: a large short is as hard to close out as a
   // large long.
   ratioOverThreshold() { $this.netSensitivity->abs() / $this.concentrationThreshold } : Float[1];
   // SIMM's multiplier, from margin-calc's function: max(1, sqrt(|S|/T)). Never below one.
   concentrationFactor() { margin_calc::mgnConcentrationFactor($this.netSensitivity, $this.concentrationThreshold) } : Float[1];
   // The add-on this row implies on a base charge the caller supplies -- also margin-calc's,
   // so the board and the margin system charge concentration the same way.
   chargeOn(baseCharge: Float[1]) { margin_calc::mgnAddOn($baseCharge, $this.concentrationRatio) } : Float[1];
   // TWO PROJECTS DEEP INTO AN AGGREGATION: margin-calc's add-on reads risk-core's factor
   // GROUP, so this is the share of the FIRM's delta in the factor that sits in one set.
   shareOfFirmFactorDelta() { $this.addOn->toOne().shareOfFirmFactorDelta() } : Float[1];
   // Whether the board's ratio still agrees with margin-calc's.
   ratioDrift() { $this.concentrationRatio - $this.addOn.concentrationRatio->toOne() } : Float[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT.
   addOn: margin_calc::MgnConcentrationAddOn[0..1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the roll-ups

// FOR: **one counterparty, on one morning, across all three sources at once** -- how much of
// its credit limit it is using, what margin it owes and has posted, and what the firm made or
// lost on it. No dependency of this project can hold this row: margin-calc has never heard of
// a credit limit, credit-limits has never heard of a margin call, and pnl-attribution has
// never heard of a counterparty. It exists only where all three closures overlap, which is
// here, and it is the reason this project is at layer 3.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbCounterpartyRollUp
[
   limitCountNotNegative: ($this.limitCount >= 0),
   agreementCountNotNegative: ($this.marginAgreementCount >= 0),
   thresholdNotNegative: ($this.totalApprovedThreshold >= 0.0)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Part 2. Both credit-limits and margin-calc key a counterparty on this string, and that
   // is the entire mechanism by which the two halves of this row are the same name.
   counterpartyId: String[1];
   counterpartyName: String[1];
   // credit-core's borrower behind the limits.
   obligorId: String[1];
   // ---- source 1: credit-limits ----
   // SMALLINT: how many limits this name is watched under.
   limitCount: Integer[1];
   totalUtilised: Float[1];
   totalApprovedThreshold: Float[1];
   // SMALLINT: how many are in excess this morning.
   openExcessCount: Integer[1];
   // The rating in force, frozen. It is the input to the credit threshold AND to the margin
   // haircut schedule, which is one of the few facts that genuinely spans two of the three.
   ratingSymbol: String[0..1];
   // ---- source 2: margin-calc ----
   marginAgreementCount: Integer[1];
   outstandingCallAmount: Float[1];
   initialMarginHeld: Float[1];
   collateralHeld: Float[1];
   // ---- source 3: pnl-attribution ----
   pnlLineCount: Integer[1];
   pnlAmount: Float[1];
   unexplainedAmount: Float[1];
   // ---- and the board's own verdict ----
   reportingCurrency: String[1];
   // SMALLINT: 1 to 5, assigned by the morning run across all three.
   riskRank: Integer[1];

   // Credit headroom, from the credit half.
   headroom() { $this.totalApprovedThreshold - $this.totalUtilised } : Float[1];
   // Utilisation as a percentage. `Integer * Float` types as Number, but both operands here
   // are Float, so the multiplication is safe and the division widens as it must.
   utilisationPct() { ($this.totalUtilised * 100.0) / $this.totalApprovedThreshold } : Float[1];
   // Collateral against margin, from the margin half.
   collateralCoverage() { $this.collateralHeld / $this.initialMarginHeld } : Float[1];
   // THE NUMBER THAT ONLY EXISTS HERE: drawn credit plus the margin owed and not yet posted,
   // less the collateral actually sitting there. Two of its three terms come from projects
   // that cannot see each other.
   netRiskAmount() { ($this.totalUtilised + $this.outstandingCallAmount) - $this.collateralHeld } : Float[1];
   // Whether this name is genuinely present in all three sources. A row missing one of them
   // is a roll-up of two things wearing the name of three.
   spansAllThreeSources() { (($this.limitCount > 0) && ($this.marginAgreementCount > 0)) && ($this.pnlLineCount > 0) } : Boolean[1];
   // The credit half re-read out of our own aggregation rather than trusted on this row.
   utilisationFromGroup() { $this.limitTotal.totalUtilised->toOne() } : Float[1];
   // And the drift between the two, which is the roll-up's own consistency screen.
   utilisationDrift() { $this.totalUtilised - $this.limitTotal.totalUtilised->toOne() } : Float[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT, TWO WAYS, ON ONE CLASS: every credit limit this name is watched under,
   // and every margin agreement it has signed. Both are [*] because a counterparty has many
   // of each, and neither dependency can reach the other.
   creditLimits: credit_limits::ClmCreditLimit[*];
   marginAgreements: margin_calc::MgnMarginAgreement[*];
   // INTO OUR OWN AGGREGATION.
   limitTotal: risk_dashboard::RdbCounterpartyLimitTotal[0..1];
}

// FOR: one desk's morning in one row -- what it made, how much of that is unexplained, what
// margin it is being charged and how close its limits are. The cut a desk head reads, as
// against the counterparty cut a credit officer reads, off the same three sources.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbDeskRollUp
[
   exceptionCountNotNegative: ($this.exceptionCount >= 0),
   marginNotNegative: ($this.marginCharged >= 0.0)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Parts 2 and 3: the account, two columns as everywhere else.
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   // The attribution run the P&L half came from, carried so the join into pnl-attribution's
   // account-day group has all four of its columns.
   attributionId: String[1];
   deskCode: String[1];
   deskName: String[1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8): the P&L half.
   totalPnl: Float[1];
   unexplainedAmount: Float[1];
   // The margin half.
   marginCharged: Float[1];
   // The credit half, as a percentage rather than an amount: a desk's limits are several and
   // the worst one is what matters.
   worstUtilisationPct: Float[1];
   // SMALLINT: what the desk has to deal with today.
   exceptionCount: Integer[1];
   ownerId: String[1];
   reportingCurrency: String[1];

   // What the six causes did account for.
   explainedPnl() { $this.totalPnl - $this.unexplainedAmount } : Float[1];
   // And what share they missed.
   unexplainedPct() { $this.unexplainedAmount / $this.totalPnl } : Float[1];
   // Return on the margin the desk is charged -- the only number on this board that puts the
   // P&L half and the margin half in one fraction.
   returnOnMargin() { $this.totalPnl / $this.marginCharged } : Float[1];
   // Whether the desk is near any of its limits at all.
   isNearLimit() { $this.worstUtilisationPct >= 90.0 } : Boolean[1];
   // The P&L half read straight out of pnl-attribution's own account-day GROUP.
   pnlFromSource() { $this.accountTotal->toOne().totalPnl() } : Float[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT, INTO A GROUP.
   accountTotal: pnl_attribution::PnlAccountDayTotal[0..1];
   owner: risk_dashboard::RdbOwner[0..1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- panel 6: the queue

// FOR: **the exceptions that need a decision today** -- the base of a filter-subtype family
// and the only part of this board that is not a report. An exception has an amount, a
// threshold it passed, an owner, a time it is due by, and a decision or the absence of one.
// One table serves four types: the base and three subtypes, discriminated on DOMAIN_CODE,
// which is what makes "this is a limit exception" a type statement rather than a convention.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbException
[
   severityInRange: (($this.severity >= 1) && ($this.severity <= 5)),
   thresholdNotNegative: ($this.thresholdAmount >= 0.0)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Part 2.
   exceptionId: String[1];
   // The tile that raised it. An exception with no tile came from nowhere and can be
   // justified by nothing.
   tileId: String[1];
   // LIMIT, MARGIN or EXPLAIN -- the discriminator the three subtypes filter on.
   domainCode: String[1];
   // What kind of exception it is within its domain, e.g. OVER_LIMIT, UNSETTLED_CALL,
   // RESIDUAL_PCT.
   exceptionCode: String[1];
   // The one line a CRO reads before deciding whether to read the rest.
   title: String[1];
   // SMALLINT: 1 informational, 5 blocks the morning sign-off.
   severity: Integer[1];
   // THE OWNER. Not a team.
   ownerId: String[1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8): what is at stake, and the level it passed.
   amount: Float[1];
   thresholdAmount: Float[1];
   currencyCode: String[1];
   raisedAt: DateTime[1];
   // THE DEADLINE. An exception with no due time is a list entry, not an obligation.
   dueBy: DateTime[1];
   // Absent until somebody decides. The null test the open-exception filter screens on.
   decidedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   // Set when this exception is yesterday's, carried forward unresolved. The single most
   // useful column on the panel: a queue of new items is a busy morning, and a queue of old
   // ones is a control failure.
   carriedFromDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   // OPEN, ACKD, DEFR, CLSD.
   statusCode: String[1];

   isOpen() { $this.decidedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   isCritical() { $this.severity >= 4 } : Boolean[1];
   // How far past the threshold it went, in money.
   overThreshold() { $this.amount - $this.thresholdAmount } : Float[1];
   // And as a ratio. Above 1.0 by construction, since a smaller amount raises nothing.
   breachRatio() { $this.amount / $this.thresholdAmount } : Float[1];
   // Yesterday's, still here.
   isCarriedOver() { $this.carriedFromDate->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // Open, critical or carried over: the filter a CRO actually applies to the queue.
   needsDecisionToday() { $this.decidedOn->isEmpty() && (($this.severity >= 4) || $this.carriedFromDate->isNotEmpty()) } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether the tile that raised it is one that can stop the morning sign-off.
   isBlocking() { ($this.severity >= 5) && $this.tile->toOne().isBlocking } : Boolean[1];
   // This exception's share of its owner's whole queue for the day, read out of the
   // aggregation rather than by summing the owner's other exceptions.
   shareOfOwnerQueue() { $this.amount / $this.ownerQueue.totalAmount->toOne() } : Float[1];

   tile: risk_dashboard::RdbTile[0..1];
   owner: risk_dashboard::RdbOwner[0..1];
   snapshot: risk_dashboard::RdbTileSnapshot[0..1];
   escalations: risk_dashboard::RdbEscalation[*];
   decisions: risk_dashboard::RdbDecision[*];
   // INTO THE TWO AGGREGATIONS this table is grouped into.
   ownerQueue: risk_dashboard::RdbOwnerQueueTotal[0..1];
   severityTotal: risk_dashboard::RdbSeverityTotal[0..1];
}

// FOR: an exception raised by the limit panel. Subtype on `~filter RdbLimitExceptionRows`;
// adds the two ids that make it actionable -- which limit, and which excess if credit-limits
// has raised one. A margin exception has no use for either and never carries one.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbLimitException extends risk_dashboard::RdbException
{
   // credit-limits' anchor.
   creditLimitId: String[1];
   // Its excess, where the crossing has been recorded as one.
   excessId: String[0..1];

   // Whether the credit system has caught up with the board.
   hasRecordedExcess() { $this.excessId->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // The limit as one string for the queue line.
   limitLabel() { 'LIMIT ' + $this.creditLimitId } : String[1];
}

// FOR: an exception raised by the margin panel. Subtype on `~filter RdbMarginExceptionRows`;
// adds the agreement and, where the exception is about a requirement rather than a call, the
// netting set it was computed on.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbMarginException extends risk_dashboard::RdbException
{
   // margin-calc's agreement.
   agreementId: String[1];
   // Its netting set, where the exception is about the requirement.
   marginPortfolioId: String[0..1];

   isPortfolioLevel() { $this.marginPortfolioId->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   agreementLabel() { 'CSA ' + $this.agreementId } : String[1];
}

// FOR: an exception raised by the explain panel. Subtype on `~filter RdbExplainExceptionRows`;
// adds the attribution run and, where the residual is on one security rather than a whole
// book, the instrument.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbExplainException extends risk_dashboard::RdbException
{
   // pnl-attribution's run.
   attributionId: String[1];
   // The security, where the residual is a single-name one.
   instrumentId: String[0..1];

   isSingleName() { $this.instrumentId->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   runLabel() { 'ATTR ' + $this.attributionId } : String[1];
}

// FOR: who was told about an exception, when, and whether they answered. One row per rung,
// because an escalation that overwrites the previous one destroys the evidence that the first
// person did not reply -- which is usually the point.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbEscalation
[
   seqIsPositive: ($this.escalationSeq >= 1),
   levelIsPositive: ($this.escalationLevel >= 1)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   exceptionId: String[1];
   // Part 2: the rung.
   escalationSeq: Integer[1];
   // Who it went to.
   toOwnerId: String[1];
   // SMALLINT: 1 desk, 2 business, 3 CRO.
   escalationLevel: Integer[1];
   // EMAIL, PAGER, CALL, TICKET.
   channelCode: String[1];
   raisedAt: DateTime[1];
   // Absent until somebody answers, which is the whole state this class records.
   acknowledgedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   note: String[0..1];

   isOutstanding() { $this.acknowledgedAt->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   isAtCroLevel() { $this.escalationLevel >= 3 } : Boolean[1];

   exception: risk_dashboard::RdbException[0..1];
   toOwner: risk_dashboard::RdbOwner[0..1];
}

// FOR: the decision itself -- what was decided about an exception, by whom, for how much and
// until when. The board's output. Everything above it is a number; this is the only class
// here that records somebody having done something about one.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbDecision
[
   seqIsPositive: ($this.decisionSeq >= 1),
   approvedNotNegative: ($this.amountApproved >= 0.0)
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key.
   exceptionId: String[1];
   // Part 2: a decision can be revised, and both survive.
   decisionSeq: Integer[1];
   // APPR (accept it), RJCT (fix it), DEFR (tomorrow), ESCL (not mine).
   decisionCode: String[1];
   decidedBy: String[1];
   decidedOn: StrictDate[1];
   // NUMERIC(24,8): how much of the exposure was accepted, which may be less than the
   // exception's amount and is why a decision is not a boolean.
   amountApproved: Float[1];
   // Absent for a permanent decision. A decision with no expiry is a policy change made by
   // accident, which is the failure mode this column exists to prevent.
   validUntil: StrictDate[0..1];
   rationale: String[0..1];

   isApproval() { $this.decisionCode == 'APPR' } : Boolean[1];
   // A deferral is a decision to look at it tomorrow, and counts as one.
   isDeferral() { $this.decisionCode == 'DEFR' } : Boolean[1];
   isTemporary() { $this.validUntil->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // What was accepted against what was at stake.
   approvedShare() { $this.amountApproved / $this.exception.amount->toOne() } : Float[1];

   exception: risk_dashboard::RdbException[0..1];
}

// FOR: the sentence somebody wrote against a tile on a morning. A dashboard without it makes
// every reader re-derive the same explanation, and the explanation is usually the only part
// that was not already in the data.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbCommentary
[
   seqIsPositive: ($this.commentSeq >= 1)
]
{
   // Parts 1 and 2: the tile and the day it is about.
   tileId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Part 3: a tile gets more than one note on a bad morning.
   commentSeq: Integer[1];
   authorId: String[1];
   commentText: String[1];
   // True once it appears on the published board rather than only in the working copy.
   isPublished: Boolean[1];
   postedAt: DateTime[1];

   // The note's provenance in one string.
   attribution() { $this.authorId + ' on ' + $this.tileId } : String[1];
   isDraft() { $this.isPublished->not() } : Boolean[1];

   tile: risk_dashboard::RdbTile[0..1];
   author: risk_dashboard::RdbOwner[0..1];
}

// FOR: something that is not an exception today and will be one soon -- a name on watch, with
// a review date and an owner. It survives the morning it was raised on, which is the
// difference between a watch list and a queue.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbWatchItem
[
   reviewAfterOpen: ($this.reviewOn >= $this.openedOn),
   severityInRange: (($this.severity >= 1) && ($this.severity <= 5))
]
{
   // The key.
   watchId: String[1];
   // The exception it grew out of, where it grew out of one.
   exceptionId: String[0..1];
   // The counterparty it is about, where it is about one.
   counterpartyId: String[0..1];
   // WATCH_RATING, WATCH_LIQUIDITY, WATCH_CONCENTRATION, WATCH_EXPLAIN.
   watchReason: String[1];
   openedOn: StrictDate[1];
   // When it must be looked at again. The column that stops a watch list becoming a graveyard.
   reviewOn: StrictDate[1];
   closedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   ownerId: String[1];
   // SMALLINT: 1 to 5.
   severity: Integer[1];
   note: String[0..1];

   isOpen() { $this.closedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether the review date has passed, against a date the caller supplies. Comparing two
   // StrictDates, which is what the class has.
   isOverdueForReview(today: StrictDate[1]) { $this.closedOn->isEmpty() && ($this.reviewOn < $today) } : Boolean[1];
   camefromException() { $this.exceptionId->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];

   exception: risk_dashboard::RdbException[0..1];
   owner: risk_dashboard::RdbOwner[0..1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- THE AGGREGATIONS
//
// Four classes rooted on store Views. In each one the ~primaryKey IS the ~groupBy, which is
// both what makes a group joinable and what one instance MEANS. These are Legend views: no
// DDL, nothing seeds them, and the engine folds the GROUP BY into its SQL. A group with no
// rows on a date does not form and is absent, rather than appearing as a total of zero --
// which on a risk dashboard is the honest answer, because zero exceptions and no data are
// different mornings and a board that shows them the same way is dangerous.

// AGGREGATE on a store view: one dashboard, one day, its tiles totalled. FOR: giving the top
// of the board its own row -- how many tiles, how much money at risk, and the worst breach on
// the page -- without reading a tile.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbTileDayTotal
{
   // The grouping, and therefore the key.
   dashboardId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // count(TILE_ID) over the group -- how many tiles the header stands on.
   tileCount: Integer[1];
   // sum(HEADLINE_VALUE). Meaningful only across tiles of one unit, which is why the money
   // total beside it is the one the header prints.
   totalHeadlineValue: Float[1];
   // sum(AMOUNT_AT_RISK) -- the board's money, in one number.
   totalAmountAtRisk: Float[1];
   // sum(EXCEPTION_COUNT).
   totalExceptionCount: Integer[1];
   // max(BREACH_RATIO) -- the worst tile on the page, which is what the header colour is.
   worstBreachRatio: Float[1];
   // min(SOURCE_ROW_COUNT) -- the emptiest tile. A board with a zero here has a tile that is
   // green because it read nothing.
   leastSourceRows: Integer[1];

   // Exceptions per tile. `/` widens to Float even on two Integers.
   exceptionsPerTile() { $this.totalExceptionCount / $this.tileCount } : Float[1];
   // Average money per tile.
   averageAmountAtRisk() { $this.totalAmountAtRisk / $this.tileCount } : Float[1];
   // Whether anything on the board is red at all.
   hasRedTile() { $this.worstBreachRatio >= 1.0 } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether some tile on the board was computed from no rows, which is the failure this
   // aggregate exists to surface: it cannot be seen from any single tile.
   hasEmptyTile() { $this.leastSourceRows == 0 } : Boolean[1];

   dashboard: risk_dashboard::RdbDashboard[0..1];
   snapshots: risk_dashboard::RdbTileSnapshot[*];
}

// AGGREGATE on a store view: one owner, one day, their whole queue. FOR: answering "what is
// on my desk this morning" in one grouped read -- and, across owners, "who is buried", which
// is a real risk about a risk dashboard and is invisible from any one exception.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbOwnerQueueTotal
{
   // The grouping, and therefore the key.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   ownerId: String[1];
   // count(EXCEPTION_ID) over the group.
   exceptionCount: Integer[1];
   // sum(AMOUNT) -- the money in this owner's queue.
   totalAmount: Float[1];
   // max(AMOUNT) -- their single worst item.
   largestAmount: Float[1];
   // max(SEVERITY) -- how bad their morning is at its worst.
   highestSeverity: Integer[1];
   // sum(THRESHOLD_AMOUNT), so the queue's aggregate breach can be stated as a ratio.
   totalThresholdAmount: Float[1];

   // The average item.
   averageAmount() { $this.totalAmount / $this.exceptionCount } : Float[1];
   // Whether one item IS the queue. Above a half, the owner has one problem and a list.
   queueConcentration() { $this.largestAmount / $this.totalAmount } : Float[1];
   // The queue's aggregate breach ratio.
   queueBreachRatio() { $this.totalAmount / $this.totalThresholdAmount } : Float[1];
   // More than a morning's work, on the firm's own rule of thumb.
   isOverloaded() { $this.exceptionCount > 10 } : Boolean[1];
   // A queue with a blocking item in it, whoever else is also waiting.
   hasBlockingItem() { $this.highestSeverity >= 5 } : Boolean[1];

   owner: risk_dashboard::RdbOwner[0..1];
   exceptions: risk_dashboard::RdbException[*];
}

// AGGREGATE on a store view: one day, one domain, one severity. FOR: the shape of the
// morning -- three criticals in margin and forty informationals in explain is a different day
// from the reverse, and the count alone cannot say which. Grouped THREE ways rather than two
// because a severity means something different in each domain.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbSeverityTotal
{
   // The grouping, and therefore the key.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   domainCode: String[1];
   severity: Integer[1];
   // count(EXCEPTION_ID) over the group.
   exceptionCount: Integer[1];
   // sum(AMOUNT).
   totalAmount: Float[1];
   // max(AMOUNT) -- the worst single item at this severity.
   largestAmount: Float[1];
   // min(AMOUNT) -- and the smallest, because a band whose largest and smallest are orders of
   // apart is not one band in any useful sense.
   smallestAmount: Float[1];

   averageAmount() { $this.totalAmount / $this.exceptionCount } : Float[1];
   // The spread within the band.
   amountSpread() { $this.largestAmount - $this.smallestAmount } : Float[1];
   // What share of the band sits in its largest item.
   largestShare() { $this.largestAmount / $this.totalAmount } : Float[1];
   // Whether this band blocks the morning sign-off.
   isBlockingBand() { $this.severity >= 5 } : Boolean[1];

   exceptions: risk_dashboard::RdbException[*];
}

// AGGREGATE on a store view: one counterparty, one day, its limit rows totalled. FOR: giving
// the counterparty roll-up its credit half as a GROUP rather than as a sum the roll-up did
// itself -- so a name's total utilisation is one grouped read, and the roll-up's own stored
// figure has something independent to be checked against.
Class risk_dashboard::RdbCounterpartyLimitTotal
{
   // The grouping, and therefore the key.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   counterpartyId: String[1];
   // count(CREDIT_LIMIT_ID) over the group.
   limitCount: Integer[1];
   // sum(UTILISED_AMOUNT).
   totalUtilised: Float[1];
   // sum(APPLIED_THRESHOLD).
   totalThreshold: Float[1];
   // sum(HEADROOM_AMOUNT) -- which can be negative, and is exactly when it matters.
   totalHeadroom: Float[1];
   // max(UTILISATION_PCT) -- the worst single limit this name is against.
   worstUtilisationPct: Float[1];
   // max(UTILISED_AMOUNT) -- and the largest single drawing.
   largestUtilised: Float[1];

   // Utilisation across every limit this name is watched under.
   utilisationRatio() { $this.totalUtilised / $this.totalThreshold } : Float[1];
   // The average limit size, which is how a name with one huge line is told from one with
   // twenty small ones.
   averageLimitSize() { $this.totalThreshold / $this.limitCount } : Float[1];
   // Whether the name's exposure is really one limit.
   isSingleLimitDriven() { ($this.largestUtilised / $this.totalUtilised) > 0.5 } : Boolean[1];
   // At least one limit near its line, whatever the aggregate says.
   hasLimitNearTheLine() { $this.worstUtilisationPct >= 90.0 } : Boolean[1];

   limitRows: risk_dashboard::RdbLimitPanelRow[*];
   rollUp: risk_dashboard::RdbCounterpartyRollUp[0..1];
}
